erestimates the integral by about half a percent of its true value of 7>3. The percentage error is (2.34375 - 7>3)>(7>3) ≈0.00446, or 0.446%."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.77833,"w":0.3766,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Simpson’s Rule: Approximations Using Parabolas"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.80299,"w":0.59308,"h":0.10639,"fs":0.01279,"n":7,"k":1,"t":"Another rule for approximating the definite integral of a continuous function results from using parabolas instead of the straight-line segments that produced trapezoids. As before, we partition the interval 3a, b4 into n subintervals of equal length h = ∆x = (b - a)>n, but this time we require that n be an even number. On each consecutive pair of intervals we approximate the curve y = ƒ(x) Ú 0 by a parabola, as shown in Figure 8.9. A typical parabola passes through three consecutive points (xi-1, yi-1), (xi, yi), and (xi+1, yi+1) on the curve."},{"x":0.16546,"y":0.808,"w":0.04579,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Parabola"},{"x":0.25346,"y":0.81084,"w":0.04306,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = f(x)"},{"x":0.08507,"y":0.83713,"w":0.15802,"h":0.02612,"fs":0.01023,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"h h y0 yn−1 yn"},{"x":0.10962,"y":0.83777,"w":0.03471,"h":0.01216,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"y1 y2"},{"x":0.20886,"y":0.86505,"w":0.06307,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xn−1 xn= b"},{"x":0.05851,"y":0.86516,"w":0.12876,"h":0.01268,"fs":0.01023,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"0 a = x0 x1 x2 h"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.89252,"w":0.24128,"h":0.04297,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 8.9 Simpson’s Rule approxi- mates short stretches of the curve with parabolas."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.91042,"w":0.59302,"h":0.03001,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Let’s calculate the shaded area beneath a parabola passing through three consecutive points. To simplify our calculations, we first take the case where x0 = -h, x1 = 0, and"}]}